We are very grateful for your support.</value></data><data name="Features_TrialLimitReachedTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Process limit reached - {0}</value></data><data name="Features_TrialLimitReachedSubTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>To process a list fully, please restart app to reset the counter or purchase premium version.</value></data><data name="Features_TrialLimitSticky" xml:space="preserve"><value>Limit: {0}</value></data><data name="Premium_UnlimitedSubtitle1" xml:space="preserve"><value>Manage unlimited files</value></data><data name="Premium_UnlimitedSubtitle2" xml:space="preserve"><value>Remove the limit of {0} files.</value></data><data name="Info_ResetNewFileSettings" xml:space="preserve"><value>Set new file's settings</value></data><data name="Info_ResetNewFileSettingsDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>Reset all settings to default for the next files.</value></data><data name="Info_Metadata" xml:space="preserve"><value>Metadata</value></data><data name="Info_MetadataDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>Metadata contains information about the device took the picture, user location, camera settings and camera model and so on, it can include EXIF, IPTC, XMP, ... of a picture.
Removing metadata strips out all the mentioned information to reduce file size and guard your information that can be extracted from the picture.</value></data><data name="Info_MetadataExif" xml:space="preserve"><value>EXIF metadata</value></data><data name="Info_MetadataExifDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>EXIF (Exchangeable Image File) contains information about the device took the picture, user location, camera settings and camera model and so on, it can include EXIF, IPTC, XMP, ... of a picture.
Removing metadata strips out all the mentioned information to reduce file size and guard your information that can be extracted from the picture.</value></data><data name="Info_MetadataXmp" xml:space="preserve"><value>XMP metadata</value></data><data name="Info_MetadataXmpDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>XMP (Extensible Metadata Platform) is a standard for embedding metadata in image files. It was created by Adobe and is similar to Exif metadata, but it provides more flexibility and can store a wider variety of information. XMP metadata can include things like keywords, captions, and copyright information, as well as Exif metadata like camera settings and date taken.
XMP metadata is stored in the image file in XML format and can be read and edited by various software.</value></data><data name="Info_MetadataIptc" xml:space="preserve"><value>IPTC metadata</value></data><data name="Info_MetadataIptcDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>IPTC (International Press Telecommunications Council) is a standard for adding information to digital images and other media files. It is often used by professional photographers and news organizations to provide information about an image, such as the photographer's name, copyright information, and keywords that describe the image.
IPTC metadata can include information like: Image title and caption, creator's name and contact information, copyright and rights usage information, keywords and categories, location and events, date created and digital creation date.
Like Exif and XMP, IPTC is embedded in the image file, but it uses its own specific format.</value></data><data name="Info_ResizeOption" xml:space="preserve"><value>Resize option</value></data><data name="Info_ResizeOptionDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>• Fixed Width (Fixed Height): resize image to Fixed Width (Fixed Height) while keeping the aspect ratio.
• Max Width (Max Height): resize image to Max Width (Max Height) if image width &gt; Max Width (image height &gt; Max Height) while keeping the aspect ratio.
• Min Width (Min Height): resize image to Min Width (Min Height) if image height &lt; Min Width (image height &lt; Min Height) while keeping the aspect ratio.
• Percentage: resize image in percentage.
• Contain: image is scaled to maintain its aspect ratio while fitting within the image's content box. The entire object is made to fill the box, while preserving its aspect ratio.
• Cover: image is resized to maintain its aspect ratio while filling the image's entire content box. If the object's aspect ratio does not match the aspect ratio of its box, then the object will be clipped to fit.
• Fill: image is resized to fill the image's content box. The entire image will completely fill the box. If the image's aspect ratio does not match the aspect ratio of its box, then the image will be stretched to fit.</value></data><data name="Info_Transparent" xml:space="preserve"><value>Image transparent</value></data><data name="Info_TransparentDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>Keep or fill transparent area with color.</value></data><data name="Info_Level" xml:space="preserve"><value>Optimization level</value></data><data name="Info_LevelDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>The higher value provides the smaller files but more time-consuming without affect to image quality, ranges from 0 to 100.</value></data><data name="Info_BackgroundColor" xml:space="preserve"><value>Background color</value></data><data name="Info_BackgroundColorDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>Fill transparent area with color.</value></data><data name="Info_DiscardIfLargerSize" xml:space="preserve"><value>Discard output image if its size is larger than original one?</value></data><data name="Info_DiscardIfLargerSizeDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>If the original file is already compressed, when compressing again, the size of the compressed file may be larger than the size of the original file, it will be replaced with the original file.</value></data><data name="Info_OptimizeOutputFileSize" xml:space="preserve"><value>File optimization</value></data><data name="Info_OptimizeOutputFileSizeDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>Try compressing the output file to get a smaller file size.
Better compression methods take more time but save more space.</value></data><data name="Info_NumberOfColors" xml:space="preserve"><value>Image colors</value></data><data name="Info_NumberOfColorsDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>Change the color pallette of the output image. The higher the value, the wider variety of colors that the output image can display.</value></data><data name="Info_DitherMethod" xml:space="preserve"><value>Dither method</value></data><data name="Info_DitherMethodDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>Dithering refers to the method of simulating colors not available in color palette. Images with continuous-tone color (especially color gradients) may require dithering to prevent color banding.</value></data><data name="Info_WebpLossless" xml:space="preserve"><value>WebP lossless</value></data><data name="Info_WebpLosslessDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>Whether the output image should be lossless or lossy.</value></data><data name="Info_WebpMethod" xml:space="preserve"><value>Method</value></data><data name="Info_WebpMethodDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>The compression method to use, it controls the trade off between encoding speed and the compressed file size and quality.
When higher values are utilized, the encoder spends more time inspecting additional encoding possibilities and decide on the quality gain.</value></data><data name="Info_WebpNearLossless" xml:space="preserve"><value>Near lossless quality</value></data><data name="Info_WebpNearLosslessDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>The near lossless encoding, between 0 (max-loss) and 100 (off).</value></data><data name="Info_WebpImageHint" xml:space="preserve"><value>Image characteristics</value></data><data name="Info_WebpImageHintDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>The characteristics of image:
• Default: default preset.
• Picture: digital picture from camera, like portrait, object shooting.
• Photo: picture taken from outdoor shooting with natural lighting.
• Graph: picture with discrete tone like graph, map-tile, …</value></data><data name="Info_BrightnessWhiteBalanceSingle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Brightness and White Balance</value></data><data name="Info_BrightnessWhiteBalanceSingleDesc" xml:space="preserve"><value>These settings apply for Camera Raw Image Format only.
Disable auto brightness: disable automatic increase in brightness by histogram.
White balance: balance the color temperature in your image, select setting bellow to generate image with color as need:
• Use Camera: use RAW-embedded color profile from the photo.
• Auto: use suitable white balance mode after averaging over the entire image.
